{"abstract":"Antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) are key components of innate immunity across all domains of life. Natural and synthetic AMPs are receiving renewed attention in efforts to combat the antimicrobial resistance (AMR) crisis and the loss of antibiotic efficacy. The gram-negative pathogen Pseudomonas aeruginosa is one of the most concerning infecting bacteria in AMR, particularly in people with cystic fibrosis (CF) where respiratory infections are difficult to eradicate and associated with increased morbidity and mortality. Cationic AMPs exploit the negatively charged lipopolysaccharides (LPS) on P. aeruginosa to bind and disrupt bacterial membrane(s), causing lethal damage. P. aeruginosa modifies its LPS to evade AMP killing. Free-LPS is also a component of CF sputum and feeds pro-inflammatory cycles. Glatiramer acetate (GA) is a random peptide co-polymer—of glycine, lysine, alanine, tyrosine—used as a drug in treatment of multiple sclerosis (MS); we have previously shown GA to be an AMP which synergises with tobramycin against CF P. aeruginosa, functioning via bacterial membrane disruption. Here, we demonstrate GA’s direct binding and sequestration/neutralisation of P. aeruginosa LPS, in keeping with GA’s ability to disrupt the outer membrane. At CF-relevant LPS concentrations, however, membrane disruption by GA was not strongly inhibited. Furthermore, exposure to GA did not result in increased Lipid A modification of LPS or in increased gene expression of systems involved in AMP sensing and LPS modification. Therefore, despite the electrostatic targeting of LPS by GA as part of its activity, P. aeruginosa does not demonstrate LPS modification in its defence.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Murphy, Ronan A.","Pizzato, Jade","Cuhbertson, Leah","Sabnis, Akshay","Edwards, Andrew M.","Nolan, Laura M.","Vorup-Jensen, Thomas","Larrouy-Maumus, Gerald","Davies, Jane C."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-726803"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Davies, Jane C."],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Imperial College London"],"keyword":["Antimicrobial peptide","Antimicrobial resistance","Cystic fibrosis","Pseudomonas"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"antimicrobial peptide glatiramer acetate targets pseudomonas aeruginosa lipopolysaccharides to breach membranes without altering lipopolysaccharide modification","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s44259-024-00022-x","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-726803","identifier":[{"id":"39843948","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC11702655","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-1d24f9c3-7f15-4410-b28d-74f5fcff6aa7"],"keyword":["Pseudomonas","antimicrobial peptide","antimicrobial resistance","cystic fibrosis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-E214A2CF9E1D494D92C1C2C3E10BC6D7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s44259-024-00022-x","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"imperial college london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Imperial College London","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Biomedicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ronan A.","key":"murphypau","last":"Murphy","name":"Murphy, Ronan A.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jade","key":"pizzatopau","last":"Pizzato","name":"Pizzato, Jade","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leah","key":"cuhbertsonpau","last":"Cuhbertson","name":"Cuhbertson, Leah","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Akshay","key":"sabnispau","last":"Sabnis","name":"Sabnis, Akshay","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrew M.","key":"edwardspau","last":"Edwards","name":"Edwards, Andrew M.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura M.","key":"nolanpau","last":"Nolan","name":"Nolan, Laura M.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"vorupjensenpau","last":"Vorup-Jensen","locid":"77b03498-95a4-453b-81a8-e2c05fa0ca0c","name":"Vorup-Jensen, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-4140-6563","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gerald","key":"larrouymaumuspau","last":"Larrouy-Maumus","name":"Larrouy-Maumus, Gerald","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jane C.","key":"daviespau","last":"Davies","name":"Davies, Jane C.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39843948"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s44259-024-00022-x","issn":["27318745"],"issue":"4","journal":"Npj Antimicrobials and Resistance","paperid":"4","publisher":"Springer Nature","status":"p","title":"Npj Antimicrobials and Resistance","title_org":"npj Antimicrobials and Resistance","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-1d24f9c3-7f15-4410-b28d-74f5fcff6aa7"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Antimicrobial peptide glatiramer acetate targets Pseudomonas aeruginosa lipopolysaccharides to breach membranes without altering lipopolysaccharide modification","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-726803","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Over the last decade, EEG resting-state microstate analysis has evolved from a niche existence to a widely used and well-accepted methodology. The rapidly increasing body of empirical findings started to yield overarching patterns of associations of biological and psychological states and traits with specific microstate classes. However, currently, this cross-referencing among apparently similar microstate classes of different studies is typically done by “eyeballing” of printed template maps by the individual authors, lacking a systematic procedure. To improve the reliability and validity of future findings, we present a tool to systematically collect the actual data of template maps from as many published studies as possible and present them in their entirety as a matrix of spatial similarity. The tool also allows importing novel template maps and systematically extracting the findings associated with specific microstate maps from ongoing or published studies. The tool also allows importing novel template maps and systematically extracting the findings associated with specific microstate maps in the literature. The analysis of 40 included sets of template maps indicated that: (i) there is a high degree of similarity of template maps across studies, (ii) similar template maps were associated with converging empirical findings, and (iii) representative meta-microstates can be extracted from the individual studies. We hope that this tool will be useful in coming to a more comprehensive, objective, and overarching representation of microstate findings.","author_count":27,"contributors":["Koenig, Thomas","Diezig, Sarah","Kalburgi, Sahana Nagabhushan","Antonova, Elena","Artoni, Fiorenzo","Brechet, Lucie","Britz, Juliane","Croce, Pierpaolo","Custo, Anna","Damborská, Alena","Deolindo, Camila Sardeto","Heinrichs, Markus","Kleinert, Tobias","Liang, Zhen","Murphy, Michael M.","Nash, Kyle","Nehaniv, Chrystopher","Schiller, Bastian","Smailovic, Una","Tarailis, Povilas","Tomescu, Miralena","Toplutaş, Eren","Vellante, Federica","Zanesco, Anthony","Zappasodi, Filippo","Zou, Qihong","Michel, Christoph M."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-728017"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Koenig, Thomas"],"country":["Canada","China","Czechia","Germany","Italy","Lithuania","Romania","Sweden","Switzerland","Turkey","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Brunel University","Gabriele dAnnunzio University","Harvard University","Istanbul Medipol University","Karolinska Institutet","Karolinska University Hospital","Leibniz Research Centre for Working Environment and Human Factors","Masaryk University","Peking University","Shenzhen University","Stefan Cel Mare University","University of Alberta","University of Bern","University of Bucharest","University of Freiburg","University of Fribourg","University of Geneva","University of Miami","University of Southern California","University of Waterloo","Vilnius University"],"keyword":["EEG","Mental states","Meta analysis","Microstates","Resting state"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"eeg meta microstates towards a more objective use of resting state eeg microstate findings across studies","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10548-023-00993-6","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-728017","identifier":[{"id":"85166273643","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37515678","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10884358","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-9c53523e-468d-4fde-840b-41f6d558c533"],"keyword":["EEG","Functional brain