c.","status":"p","title":"Brain Topography","volume":"37"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85166273643"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-9c53523e-468d-4fde-840b-41f6d558c533"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"EEG-Meta-Microstates : Towards a More Objective Use of Resting-State EEG Microstate Findings Across Studies","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-728017","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Explainable AI (XAI) is pivotal for understanding complex ’black-box’ models, particularly in text analysis, where transparency is essential yet challenging. This paper introduces SIDU-TXT, an adaptation of the ’Similarity Difference and Uniqueness’ (SIDU) method, originally applied in image classification, to textual data. SIDU-TXT generates word-level heatmaps using feature activation maps, highlighting contextually important textual elements for model predictions. Given the absence of a unified standard for assessing XAI methods, to evaluate SIDU-TXT, we implement a comprehensive three-tiered evaluation framework – Functionally-Grounded, Human-Grounded, and Application-Grounded – across varied experimental setups. Our findings show SIDU-TXT’s effectiveness in sentiment analysis, outperforming benchmarks like Grad-CAM and LIME in both Functionally and Human-Grounded assessments. In a legal domain application involving complex asylum decision-making, SIDU-TXT displays competitive but not conclusive results, underscoring the nuanced expectations of domain experts. This work advances the field by offering a methodical holistic approach to XAI evaluation in NLP, urging further research to bridge the existing gap in expert expectations and refine interpretability methods for intricate applications. The study underscores the critical role of extensive evaluations in fostering AI technologies that are not only technically faithful to the model but also comprehensible and trustworthy for end-users.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Jahromi, Mohammad N.S.","Muddamsetty, Satya M.","Jarlner, Asta Sofie Stage","Høgenhaug, Anna Murphy","Gammeltoft-Hansen, Thomas","Moeslund, Thomas B."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-791236"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sidu txt an xai algorithm for nlp with a holistic assessment approach","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/jemvk%20License","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-791236","ids":["ku-f78be3a6-8150-44ea-9a28-873e413793d7"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4C1568E05476D8FBC024486CF961C932"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/jemvk%20License","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Law, Area of Research, JUR - MOBILE - Center for Global Mobility Law","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Law, Full-time Law programs, PhD programme"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Mohammad N.S.","key":"jahromipau","last":"Jahromi","name":"Jahromi, Mohammad N.S.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Satya M.","key":"muddamsettypau","last":"Muddamsetty","name":"Muddamsetty, Satya M.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Asta Sofie Stage","key":"jarlnerpau","last":"Jarlner","locid":"45b7e1d0-317f-4115-ace6-4d73fa571d74","name":"Jarlner, Asta Sofie Stage","orcid":"0000-0001-6409-637X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anna Murphy","key":"høgenhaugpau","last":"Høgenhaug","locid":"c13954df-c43d-4588-adf6-3d36914b3abb","name":"Høgenhaug, Anna Murphy","orcid":"0009-0004-6715-0561","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Thomas","key":"gammeltofthansenpau","last":"Gammeltoft-Hansen","locid":"782edd72-af25-4d5f-9795-455619228ae9","name":"Gammeltoft-Hansen, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0003-1518-137X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas B.","key":"moeslundpau","last":"Moeslund","name":"Moeslund, Thomas B.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.31235/osf.io/jemvklicense","issue":"38","pages":"25","publisher":"Mobile Working Paper Series","series":"MOBILE Working Paper Series","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f78be3a6-8150-44ea-9a28-873e413793d7"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"SIDU-TXT : An XAI algorithm for NLP with a holistic assessment approach","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-791236","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The emergence of alternative stable states in forest systems has significant implications for the functioning and structure of the terrestrial biosphere, yet empirical evidence remains scarce. Here, we combine global forest biodiversity observations and simulations to test for alternative stable states in the presence of evergreen and deciduous forest types. We reveal a bimodal distribution of forest leaf types across temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere that cannot be explained by the environment alone, suggesting signatures of alternative forest states. Moreover, we empirically demonstrate the existence of positive feedbacks in tree growth, recruitment and mortality, with trees having 4–43% higher growth rates, 14–17% higher survival rates and 4–7 times higher recruitment rates when they are surrounded by trees of their own leaf type. Simulations show that the observed positive feedbacks are necessary and sufficient to generate alternative forest states, which also lead to dependency on history (hysteresis) during ecosystem transition from evergreen to deciduous forests and vice versa. We identify hotspots of bistable forest types in evergreen-deciduous ecotones, which are likely driven by soil-related positive feedbacks. These findings are integral to predicting the distribution of forest biomes, and aid to our understanding of biodiversity, carbon turnover, and terrestrial climate feedbacks.","author_count":23,"contributors":["Zou, Yibiao","Zohner, Constantin M.","Averill, Colin","Ma, Haozhi","Merder, Julian","Berdugo, Miguel","Bialic-Murphy, Lalasia","Mo, Lidong","Brun, Philipp","Zimmermann, Niklaus E.","Liang, Jingjing","de-Miguel, Sergio","Nabuurs, Gert Jan","Reich, Peter B.","Niinements, Ulo","Dahlgren, Jonas","Kändler, Gerald","Ratcliffe, Sophia","Ruiz-Benito, Paloma","de Zavala, Miguel Angel","Crowther, Thomas W.","Johannsen, Vivian Kvist","Kepfer-Rojas, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S02-097.62-741753"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corpau":["GFBI consortium"],"corresponding_author":["Zou, Yibiao"],"country":["Australia","Estonia","Germany","Netherlands","Spain","Sweden","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Carnegie Institution of Washington","Carnegie Science","Centre Tecnològic Forestal de Catalunya","ETH Zürich","Estonian University of Life Sciences","Forest Technology Centre of Catalonia","Forstliche Versuchs- und Forschungsanstalt Baden-Württemberg","National Biodiversity Network Trust","Purdue University","Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences","Swiss Federal Institute for Forest, Snow and Landscape Research","University of Alcalá","University of Lleida","University of Michigan","University of Michigan, Ann Arbor","University of Minnesota","University of Minnesota Twin Cities","Wageningen University & Research","Western Sydney University"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"positive feedbacks and alternative stable states in forest leaf types","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-48676-5","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-097.62-741753","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11143268","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"38821957","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85195001010","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-31298b9b-9555-4937-a3da-d13422625f3f","ku-1fcd995a-e563-45fb-9fda-57c63b8808e4"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1D79CD957655D1DA58F3BC92DC931273"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"do