{"abstract":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most common pathogen infecting the lungs of people with cystic fibrosis (CF), causing both acute and chronic infections. Intrinsic and acquired antibiotic resistance, coupled with the physical barriers resulting from desiccated CF sputum, allow P. aeruginosa to colonize and persist in spite of antibiotic treatment. As well as the specific difficulties in eradicating P. aeruginosa from CF lungs, P. aeruginosa is also subject to the wider, global issue of antimicrobial resistance. Glatiramer acetate (GA) is a peptide drug, used in the treatment of multiple sclerosis (MS), which has been shown to have moderate antipseudomonal activity. Other antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) have been shown to be antibiotic resistance breakers, potentiating the activities of antibiotics when given in combination, restoring and/or enhancing antibiotic efficacy. Growth, viability, MIC determinations, and synergy analysis showed that GA improved the efficacy of tobramycin (TOB) against reference strains of P. aeruginosa, reducing TOB MICs and synergizing with the aminoglycoside. This was also the case for clinical strains from people with CF. GA significantly reduced the MIC 50 of TOB for viable cells from 1.69 mg/L (95% confidence interval [CI], 0.26 to 8.97) to 0.62 mg/L (95% CI, 0.15 to 3.94; P = 0.002) and the MIC 90 for viable cells from 7.00 mg/L (95% CI, 1.18 to 26.50) to 2.20 mg/L (95% CI, 0.99 to 15.03; P = 0.001), compared to results with TOB only. Investigation of mechanisms of GA activity showed that GA resulted in significant disruption of outer membranes, depolarization of cytoplasmic membranes, and permeabilization of P. aeruginosa and was the only agent tested (including cationic AMPs) to significantly affect all three mechanisms.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Murphy, Ronan A","Coates, Matthew","Thrane, Sophia","Sabnis, Akshay","Harrison, James","Schelenz, Silke","Edwards, Andrew M","Vorup-Jensen, Thomas","Davies, Jane C"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-236685"],"filters":{"added":"2022-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Imperial College London","King's College Hospital NHS Foundation Trust","Royal Brompton and Harefield NHS Foundation Trust"],"keyword":["Anti Bacterial Agents pharmacology","Antibiotic resistance","Antimicrobial peptides","Antimicrobial resistance","Cystic Fibrosis drug therapy","Cystic fibrosis","Divalent cations","Drug repurposing","Glatiramer Acetate","Glatiramer Acetate pharmacology","Humans","MODEL","Mechanisms","Microbial Sensitivity Tests","Multiple sclerosis","OUTER MEMBRANE","Permeability","Pseudomonas Infections drug therapy","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Synergism","Tobramycin pharmacology"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"synergistic activity of repurposed peptide drug glatiramer acetate with tobramycin against cystic fibrosis i pseudomonas aeruginosa i","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85137135596","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-236685","identifier":[{"id":"35727066","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85137135596","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC9430792","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"000814617200001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-bc085c42-c92b-429d-90c0-c9182e5e5cd0"],"keyword":["AMINOGLYCOSIDE-RESISTANCE","Anti-Bacterial Agents/pharmacology","CATIONIC ANTIMICROBIAL PEPTIDES","Cystic Fibrosis","Cystic Fibrosis/drug therapy","DIVALENT-CATIONS","Glatiramer Acetate","Glatiramer Acetate/pharmacology","Humans","INHALED TOBRAMYCIN","MECHANISMS","MODEL","MULTIPLE-SCLEROSIS","Microbial Sensitivity Tests","OUTER-MEMBRANE","PERMEABILITY","Pseudomonas Infections/drug therapy","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Tobramycin/pharmacology","antibiotic resistance","antibiotic resistance breaker","antimicrobial peptides","antimicrobial resistance","drug repurposing","synergism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E732B537BEFA548BDA00CC2CDFBE8219"],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"imperial college london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Imperial College London","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Biomedicine, Department of Biomedicine - Forskning og uddannelse, Skou-bygningen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"king s college hospital nhs foundation trust","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"King's College Hospital NHS Foundation Trust","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"royal brompton and harefield nhs foundation trust","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Royal Brompton and Harefield NHS Foundation Trust","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ronan A","key":"murphypau","last":"Murphy","name":"Murphy, Ronan A","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Matthew","key":"coatespau","last":"Coates","name":"Coates, Matthew","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sophia","key":"thranepau","last":"Thrane","locid":"70ec74ed-7641-40d4-8a4b-163157f5dbcb","name":"Thrane, Sophia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Akshay","key":"sabnispau","last":"Sabnis","name":"Sabnis, Akshay","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"James","key":"harrisonpau","last":"Harrison","name":"Harrison, James","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Silke","key":"schelenzpau","last":"Schelenz","name":"Schelenz, Silke","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrew M","key":"edwardspau","last":"Edwards","name":"Edwards, Andrew M","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"vorupjensenpau","last":"Vorup-Jensen","locid":"77b03498-95a4-453b-81a8-e2c05fa0ca0c","name":"Vorup-Jensen, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-4140-6563","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Jane C","key":"daviespau","last":"Davies","name":"Davies, Jane C","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35727066"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1128/spectrum.00813-22","issn":["21650497"],"issue":"4","journal":"Microbiology Spectrum","paperid":"e00813-22","publisher":"American Society for Microbiology","status":"p","title":"Microbiology Spectrum","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85137135596"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-bc085c42-c92b-429d-90c0-c9182e5e5cd0"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Synergistic Activity of Repurposed Peptide Drug Glatiramer Acetate with Tobramycin against Cystic Fibrosis <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i>","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-236685","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"DIII-D physics research addresses critical challenges for the operation of ITER and the next generation of fusion energy devices. This is done through a focus on innovations to provide solutions for high performance long pulse operation, coupled with fundamental plasma physics understanding and model validation, to drive scenario development by integrating high performance core and boundary plasmas. Substantial increases in off-axis current drive efficiency from an innovative top launch system for EC power, and in pressure broadening for Alfven eigenmode control from a co-/counter-I p steerable off-axis neutral beam, all improve the prospects for optimization of future long pulse/steady state high performance tokamak operation. Fundamental studies into the modes that drive the evolution of the pedestal pressure profile and electron vs ion heat flux validate predictive models of pedestal recovery after ELMs. Understanding the physics mechanisms of ELM control and density pumpout by 3D magnetic perturbation fields leads to confident predictions for ITER and future devices. Validated modeling of high-Z shattered pellet injection for disruption mitigation, runaway electron dissipation, and techniques for disruption prediction and avoidance including machine learning, give confidence in handling disruptivity for future devices. For the non-nuclear phase of ITER, two actuators are identified to lower the L-H threshold power in hydrogen plasmas. With this physics understanding and suite of capabilities, a high poloidal beta optimized-core scenario with an internal transport barrier that projects nearly to Q = 10 in ITER at ∼8 MA was coupled to a detached divertor, and a near super H-mode optimized-pedestal scenario with co-I p beam injection was coupled to a radiative divertor. The hybrid core scenario was achieved directly, without the need for anomalous current diffusion, using off-axis current drive actuators. Also, a controller to assess proximity to stability limits and regulate β N in the ITER baseline scenario, based on plasma response to probing 3D fields, was demonstrated. Finally, innovative tokamak operation using a negative triangularity shape showed many attractive features for future pilot plant operation.","author_count":813,"contributors":["Fenstermacher, M. E.","Abbate, J.","Abe, S.","Abrams, T.","Adams, M.","Adamson, B.","Aiba, N.","Akiyama, T.","Aleynikov, P.","Allen, E.","Allen, S.","Anand, H.","Anderson, J.","Andrew, Y.","Andrews, T.","Appelt, D.","Arbon, R.","Ashikawa, N.","Ashourvan, A.","Aslin, M.","Asnis, Y.","Austin, M.","Ayala, D.","Bak, J.","Bandyopadhyay, I.","Banerjee, S.","Barada, K.","Bardoczi, L.","Barr, J.","Bass, E.","Battaglia, D.","Battey, A.","Baumgartner, W.","Baylor, L.","Beckers, J.","Beidler, M.","Belli, E.","Berkery, J.","Bernard, T.","Bertelli, N.","Beurskens, M.","Bielajew, R.","Bilgili, S.","Biswas, B.","Blondel, S.","Boedo, J.","Bogatu, I.","Boivin, R.","Bolzonella, T.","Bongard, M.","Bonnin, X.","Bonoli, P.","Bonotto, M.","Bortolon, A.","Bose, S.","Bosviel, N.","Bouwmans, S.","Boyer, M.","Boyes, W.","Bradley, L.","Brambila, R.","Brennan, D.","Bringuier, S.","Brodsky, L.","Brookman, M.","Brooks, J.","Brower, D.","Brown, G.","Brown, W.","Burke, M.","Burrell, K.","Butler, K.","Buttery, R.","Bykov, I.","Byrne, P.","Cacheris, A.","Callahan, K.","Callen, J.","Campbell, G.","Candy, J.","Canik, J.","Cano-Megias, P.","Cao, N.","Carayannopoulos, L.","Carlstrom, T.","Carrig, W.","Carter, T.","Cary, W.","Casali, L.","Cengher, M.","Cespedes Paz, G.","Chaban, R.","Chan, V.","Chapman, B.","Char, I.","Chattopadhyay, A.","Chen, R.","Chen, J.","Chen, X.","Chen, X.","Chen, J.","Chen, M.","Chen, J.","Chen, Z.","Choi, M.","Choi, W.","Choi, G.","Chousal, L.","Chrobak, C.","Chrystal, C.","Chung, Y.","Churchill, R.","Cianciosa, M.","Clark, J.","Clement, M.","Coda, S.","Cole, A.","Collins, C.","Conlin, W.","Cooper, A.","Cordell, J.","Coriton, B.","Cote, T.","Cothran, J.","Creely, A.","Crocker, N.",