{"abstract":"Measurement of glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) has been the traditional method for assessing glycemic control. However, it does not reflect intra- and interday glycemic excursions that may lead to acute events (such as hypoglycemia) or postprandial hyperglycemia, which have been linked to both microvascular and macrovascular complications. Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM), either from real-time use (rtCGM) or intermittently viewed (iCGM), addresses many of the limitations inherent in HbA1c testing and self-monitoring of blood glucose. Although both provide themeans to move beyond the HbA1c measurement as the sole marker of glycemic control, standardized metrics for analyzing CGM data are lacking. Moreover, clear criteria for matching people with diabetes to themost appropriate glucose monitoring methodologies, as well as standardized advice about howbest to use the new information they provide, have yet to be established. In February 2017, the Advanced Technologies & Treatments for Diabetes (ATTD) Congress convened an international panel of physicians, researchers, and individuals with diabetes who are expert in CGM technologies to address these issues. This article summarizes the ATTD consensus recommendations and represents the current understanding of how CGM results can affect outcomes.","author_count":34,"contributors":["Danne, Thomas","Nimri, Revital","Battelino, Tadej","Bergenstal, Richard M.","Close, Kelly L.","DeVries, J. Hans","Garg, Satish","Heinemann, Lutz","Hirsch, Irl","Amiel, Stephanie A.","Beck, Roy","Bosi, Emanuele","Buckingham, Bruce","Cobelli, Claudio","Dassau, Eyal","Doyle, Francis J.","Heller, Simon","Hovorka, Roman","Jia, Weiping","Jones, Tim","Kordonouri, Olga","Kovatchev, Boris","Kowalski, Aaron","Laffel, Lori","Maahs, David","Murphy, Helen R.","Nørgaard, Kirsten","Parkin, Christopher G.","Renard, Eric","Saboo, Banshi","Scharf, Mauro","Tamborlane, William V.","Weinzimer, Stuart A.","Phillip, Moshe"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-445882"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"international consensus on use of continuous glucose monitoring","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc17-1600","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-445882","identifier":[{"id":"85036620716","type":"scopus"},{"id":"29162583","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-57b2149e-48e4-41be-8322-d419da0f3c9a"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-93B7EAB2B5FE1B8361085511CC3D2D64"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/189362437/1631.full.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc17-1600","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Internal Medicine: Endocrinology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Thomas","key":"dannepau","last":"Danne","name":"Danne, Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Revital","key":"nimripau","last":"Nimri","name":"Nimri, Revital","ra