{"abstract":"The continuing rise in atmospheric CO2 concentrations is driving a rapid increase in ambient temperatures. The accompanying environmental changes will progressively reduce the area of arable land available and thus pose a grave threat to global food security. The situation is exacerbated both by exponential population growth and increased demand for crop plants as sources of renewable energy or high-value products. The foreseeable intensification of competition between agronomical and industrial use makes it imperative that the available supply of cropland be used more efficiently. During the Green Revolution that began in the 1960s, significant increases in yield could be achieved by more effective farming strategies, innovations in fertilization, and the introduction of dwarfing genes into important crop species like rice (Oryza sativa) and wheat (Triticum aestivum). The last resulted in a shift of carbon allocation within the plant from the vegetative tissue to the grain. The stunted growth phenotypes of the new varieties also reduced yield losses caused by fertilization-based lodging effects. In recent years, conventional breeding endeavors have been unable to maintain the resulting high rates of grain yield increase per unit area of arable land, and the beneficial effects associated with the Green Revolution have virtually ceased due to the already widespread cultivation of improved varieties. Currently the most promising approaches to improving crop yield appear to be those based on the genetic engineering of developmental or bioenergetic processes, such as photosynthesis. These approaches offer the prospect of a renewal of the Green Revolution, which is urgently required tomeet the continuously increasing demand for superior high-yield crop varieties for human sustenance and industrial applications in the future. This article will highlight genetic approaches to the remodeling of the primary metabolism of photosynthesis with a view to establishing the basis for a sustainable increase in yield and biomass production in crop plants.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Pribil, Mathias","Leister, Dario Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-427657"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"photosynthesis","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-427657","identifier":[{"id":"85042796145","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-39809c4f-1472-4b82-ac93-1dff14c54a06"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-74B512C421830D289D817BEC898F8822"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Molecular Plant Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"ludwig maximilian university of munich","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Mathias","key":"pribilpau","last":"Pribil","locid":"755dd902-1537-4279-8630-7098d49ce6ee","name":"Pribil, Mathias","orcid":"0000-0002-9174-9548","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Dario Michael","key":"leisterpau","last":"Leister","locid":"7f1c95f6-28ae-4ff0-ac8c-0b61b460aa01","name":"Leister, Dario Michael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Brian","key":"thomaspedh","last":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Brian","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Brian G.","key":"murraypedh","last":"Murray","name":"Murray, Brian G.","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Denis J.","key":"murphypedh","last":"Murphy","name":"Murphy, Denis J.","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/b978-0-12-394807-6.00156-8","edition":"2.","isbn":["9780123948083"],"pages":"90-95","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Encyclopedia of applied plant sciences","volume":"1"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85042796145"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-39809c4f-1472-4b82-ac93-1dff14c54a06"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Photosynthesis","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-427657","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Early detection and intervention are likely to be the most effective means for reducing morbidity and mortality of human cancer. However, development of methods for noninvasive detection of early-stage tumors has remained a challenge. We have developed an approach called targeted error correction sequencing (TEC-Seq) that allows ultrasensitive direct evaluation of sequence changes in circulating cell-free DNA using massively parallel sequencing. We have used this approach to examine 58 cancer-related genes encompassing 81 kb. Analysis of plasma from 44 healthy individuals identified genomic changes related to clonal hematopoiesis in 16% of asymptomatic individuals but no alterations in driver genes related to solid cancers. Evaluation of 200 patients with colorectal, breast, lung, or ovarian cancer detected somatic mutations in the plasma of 71, 59, 59, and 68%, respectively, of patients with stage I or II disease. Analyses of mutations in the circulation revealed high concordance with alterations in the tumors of these patients. In patients with resectable colorectal cancers, higher amounts of preoperative circulating tumor DNA were associated with disease recurrence and decreased overall survival. These analyses provide a broadly applicable approach for noninvasive detection of early-stage tumors that may be useful for screening and management of patients with cancer.","author_count":37,"contributors":["Phallen, Jillian","Sausen, Mark","Adleff, Vilmos","Leal, Alessandro","Hruban, Carolyn","White, James","Anagnostou, Valsamo","Fiksel, Jacob","Cristiano, Stephen","Papp, Eniko","Speir, Savannah","Reinert, Thomas","Ørntoft, Mai-Britt Worm","Woodward, Brian D","Murphy, Derek","Parpart-Li, Sonya","Riley, David","Nesselbush, Monica","Sengamalay, Naomi","Georgiadis, Andrew","Li, Qing Kay","Madsen, Mogens Rørbæk","Mortensen, Frank Viborg","Huiskens, Joost","Punt, Cornelis","van Grieken, Nicole","Fijneman, Remond","Meijer, Gerrit","Husain, Hatim","Scharpf, Robert B","Diaz, Luis A","Jones, Siân","Angiuoli, Sam","Ørntoft, Torben Falck","Nielsen, Hans Jørgen","Andersen, Claus Lindbjerg","Velculescu, Victor E"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-020709"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Amsterdam UMC","Johns Hopkins University","Netherlands Cancer Institute","University of California at San Diego"],"keyword":["Blood Cells metabolism","Case Control Studies","Cell Free Nucleic Acids blood","Circulating Tumor DNA blood","Disease Progression","Early Detection of Cancer methods","Female","Genes Neoplasm","Humans","Journal Article","Mutation genetics","Neoplasm Staging","Neoplasms blood","Preoperative Care","Sequence Analysis DNA","Treatment Outcome"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"direct detection of early stage cancers using circulating tumor dna","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/scitranslmed.aan2415","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-020709","identifier":[{"id":"28814544","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6714979","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85027717075","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-6dfa3c46-9425-4858-ac61-877a09e57fbf","ku-bb236fd1-8d5b-4a86-ae71-37ac25d9703e"],"keyword":["Blood Cells/metabolism","Case-Control Studies","Cell-Free Nucleic Acids/blood","Circulating Tumor DNA/blood","Disease Progression","Early Detection of Cancer/methods","Female","Genes, Neoplasm","Humans","Journal Article","M